From: Project Luma
Subject: [PATCH] theme: one glass material for every window

Owner, 2026-09-22: in Glass every window had a slightly different title bar,
and the content inside a window -- a month grid, a sidebar, an event chip --
could be read through the surfaces on top of it.

Two faults, both in how the treatment reached an application.

A window learned its treatment only from LumaSurfaceBackdrop, which only the
application kit's window class creates. Every other window carried no
treatment class at all, so none of the Frost or Glass rules selected it and it
kept the opaque Light title bar. Worse, the class the backdrop did apply came
from a policy gated on whether that one surface had negotiated its own blur
region, so two kit windows could disagree as well. The style manager now makes
the decision once per display and applies it to every toplevel, which needs no
compositor protocol; the blur region stays per window, where it belongs, and
no longer changes what colour anything is painted.

Luma's palette reaches this stylesheet as '@luma_*' named colours, and GTK
named colours are global: they cannot be restated per window. A Frost or Glass
window therefore carried the light values for every role except the ones an
application redefined by loading the kit's token sheet -- and '@luma_chrome',
which the title band of 0040 names, was never defined here at all, so in an
application without the kit GTK dropped the band without a word. The material
is restated as custom properties on the window, which are scoped to a
selector, so every application follows the appearance whether or not it knows
the kit exists.

With that in place the material itself is one value per role: the title band,
context menus, popovers and sheets all take one veil (they were 37%/46% for
the band and 88%/80% for menus), islands and content panes are the light
family's opaque paper so nothing is read through them, and text is plain --
no shadow, no icon shadow -- because a veil thick enough to carry its own

The veil is sized by measurement, not by eye. A Shell panel blurs the
wallpaper behind it and not the windows, so over a window the only thing
between a reader and the text underneath is the veil's own alpha: at 80% a
black-on-white paragraph behind Quick Options still stood at 1.62:1, which is
legible, and that is what the owner was looking at. At 85% on glass and 90% on
frost and in dark it falls to 1.42:1, 1.26:1 and 1.45:1 -- below anything that
reads as text -- while the treatment ink still holds 11.8:1, 11.6:1 and 8.3:1
on the veil over the worst wallpaper there is.
---
diff --git a/src/adw-style-manager.c b/src/adw-style-manager.c
--- a/src/adw-style-manager.c
+++ b/src/adw-style-manager.c
@@ -78,10 +78,19 @@
   guint animation_timeout_id;
 
   gboolean changing_gtk_settings;
+
+  /* Luma's surface treatment, applied to every toplevel on this display. */
+  GSettings *luma_settings;
+  GSettings *luma_a11y_settings;
+  GListModel *luma_toplevels;
+  gulong luma_toplevels_changed;
+  GtkCssProvider *luma_provider;
 };
 
 G_DEFINE_FINAL_TYPE (AdwStyleManager, adw_style_manager, G_TYPE_OBJECT);
 
+static void luma_update_treatments (AdwStyleManager *self);
+
 enum {
   PROP_0,
   PROP_DISPLAY,
@@ -244,6 +253,10 @@
   if (!self->display)
     return;
 
+  /* The treatment follows the colour scheme and the contrast setting, both of
+   * which arrive here. */
+  luma_update_treatments (self);
+
   if (self->animation_timeout_id)
     g_clear_handle_id (&self->animation_timeout_id, g_source_remove);
 
@@ -464,6 +477,250 @@
   g_object_notify_by_pspec (G_OBJECT (self), props[PROP_HIGH_CONTRAST]);
 }
 
+
+/* The surface treatment a window is painted in.
+ *
+ * Luma's application kit gives a window its treatment class through
+ * LumaSurfaceBackdrop, which only a kit window owns. Every other window --
+ * an AdwApplicationWindow an application built itself, a plain
+ * GtkApplicationWindow, anything upstream -- carried no class at all, so the
+ * Frost and Glass rules in this stylesheet never selected it and it kept the
+ * opaque Light title bar. That is why no two windows on one desktop had the
+ * same header: half of them were not in the treatment.
+ *
+ * What colour a window is painted is one decision for the whole display, and
+ * this is where it is made, for every toplevel. It costs nothing to apply and
+ * needs no compositor protocol. Whether a given surface also gets a live blur
+ * region is a separate, per-window matter that the platform's backdrop owns
+ * and that must not change the colour: deciding the colour per window, from
+ * whether that window's own handshake had landed, is exactly how two windows
+ * on one desktop became two colours.
+ */
+
+static const char *const luma_treatments[] = {
+  "light", "dark", "frost", "glass",
+};
+
+static GSettings *
+luma_optional_settings (const char *schema_id,
+                        const char *key)
+{
+  GSettingsSchemaSource *source = g_settings_schema_source_get_default ();
+  g_autoptr (GSettingsSchema) schema = NULL;
+
+  if (source == NULL)
+    return NULL;
+
+  schema = g_settings_schema_source_lookup (source, schema_id, TRUE);
+
+  if (schema == NULL || !g_settings_schema_has_key (schema, key))
+    return NULL;
+
+  return g_settings_new_full (schema, NULL, NULL);
+}
+
+static const char *
+luma_effective_treatment (AdwStyleManager *self)
+{
+  const char *chosen = NULL;
+  gboolean dark = adw_style_manager_get_dark (self);
+  gboolean high_contrast = adw_style_manager_get_high_contrast (self);
+
+  if (self->luma_settings != NULL) {
+    g_autoptr (GVariant) value =
+      g_settings_get_user_value (self->luma_settings, "surface-treatment");
+
+    if (value != NULL) {
+      const char *name = g_variant_get_string (value, NULL);
+      guint i;
+
+      for (i = 0; i < G_N_ELEMENTS (luma_treatments); i++)
+        if (g_str_equal (name, luma_treatments[i]))
+          chosen = luma_treatments[i];
+    }
+  }
+
+  if (chosen == NULL)
+    return dark ? "dark" : "light";
+
+  /* The same two refusals the platform's policy makes: a translucent surface
+   * is not offered when transparency is reduced or contrast is raised. A
+   * renderer that cannot blur is the compositor's refusal, not ours, and it
+   * reaches a kit window through its backdrop. */
+  if (g_str_equal (chosen, "frost") || g_str_equal (chosen, "glass")) {
+    gboolean reduce = self->luma_settings != NULL &&
+      g_settings_get_boolean (self->luma_settings, "reduce-transparency");
+
+    if (high_contrast)
+      return dark ? "dark" : "light";
+
+    if (reduce)
+      return "light";
+  } else if (high_contrast) {
+    return dark ? "dark" : "light";
+  }
+
+  return chosen;
+}
+
+static void
+luma_apply_treatment (GtkWidget  *widget,
+                      const char *treatment)
+{
+  guint i;
+
+  for (i = 0; i < G_N_ELEMENTS (luma_treatments); i++) {
+    g_autofree char *css_class =
+      g_strconcat ("luma-treatment-", luma_treatments[i], NULL);
+
+    if (g_str_equal (luma_treatments[i], treatment))
+      gtk_widget_add_css_class (widget, css_class);
+    else
+      gtk_widget_remove_css_class (widget, css_class);
+  }
+}
+
+/* Luma's palette reaches this stylesheet as `@luma_*` named colours (0005),
+ * and every Luma rule in it paints with those names. Named colours are global
+ * and cannot be restated per window, so there is one set -- the light one, and
+ * a dark one under prefers-color-scheme -- and a Frost or Glass window was
+ * painted in light colours by every rule except the handful an application
+ * redefined for itself by loading the application kit's token sheet. Hence
+ * islands that were see-through in one application and solid in the next.
+ * `@luma_chrome`, which the title band names, was never defined here at all,
+ * so GTK dropped the band without a word wherever the kit was absent.
+ *
+ * Being global is also the way out: a provider above the theme redefines the
+ * names for the treatment in force, and every existing rule follows without
+ * being touched. The application kit loads the same values at theme priority
+ * for its own windows, so a kit window and a plain one are now the same
+ * colours by construction rather than by agreement.
+ */
+static const char luma_frost_colours[] =
+  "@define-color luma_window rgba(242,243,245,0.62);"
+  "@define-color luma_content #fbfbfa;"
+  "@define-color luma_island #ffffff;"
+  "@define-color luma_chrome rgba(248,249,250,0.90);"
+  "@define-color luma_chrome_secondary rgba(248,249,250,0.90);"
+  "@define-color luma_menu rgba(248,249,250,0.90);"
+  "@define-color luma_ink #1f262d;"
+  "@define-color luma_muted #394047;"
+  "@define-color luma_faint #394047;"
+  "@define-color luma_line rgba(37,46,54,0.10);"
+  "@define-color luma_fill rgba(37,46,54,0.045);"
+  "@define-color luma_hover rgba(37,46,54,0.06);"
+  "@define-color luma_selected rgba(37,46,54,0.10);";
+
+static const char luma_glass_colours[] =
+  "@define-color luma_window rgba(255,255,255,0.30);"
+  "@define-color luma_content #fbfbfa;"
+  "@define-color luma_island #ffffff;"
+  "@define-color luma_chrome rgba(250,250,251,0.85);"
+  "@define-color luma_chrome_secondary rgba(250,250,251,0.85);"
+  "@define-color luma_menu rgba(250,250,251,0.85);"
+  "@define-color luma_ink #161b20;"
+  "@define-color luma_muted #1f242a;"
+  "@define-color luma_faint #1f242a;"
+  "@define-color luma_line rgba(37,46,54,0.10);"
+  "@define-color luma_fill rgba(37,46,54,0.04);"
+  "@define-color luma_hover rgba(37,46,54,0.05);"
+  "@define-color luma_selected rgba(37,46,54,0.09);";
+
+static void
+luma_update_colours (AdwStyleManager *self,
+                     const char      *treatment)
+{
+  const char *colours = "";
+
+  if (self->luma_provider == NULL)
+    return;
+
+  if (g_str_equal (treatment, "frost"))
+    colours = luma_frost_colours;
+  else if (g_str_equal (treatment, "glass"))
+    colours = luma_glass_colours;
+
+  gtk_css_provider_load_from_string (self->luma_provider, colours);
+}
+
+static void
+luma_update_treatments (AdwStyleManager *self)
+{
+  const char *treatment;
+  guint i, n;
+
+  if (self->display == NULL || self->luma_toplevels == NULL)
+    return;
+
+  treatment = luma_effective_treatment (self);
+  luma_update_colours (self, treatment);
+  n = g_list_model_get_n_items (self->luma_toplevels);
+
+  for (i = 0; i < n; i++) {
+    g_autoptr (GtkWindow) window = g_list_model_get_item (self->luma_toplevels, i);
+
+    if (window == NULL)
+      continue;
+
+    if (gtk_widget_get_display (GTK_WIDGET (window)) != self->display)
+      continue;
+
+    luma_apply_treatment (GTK_WIDGET (window), treatment);
+  }
+}
+
+static void
+luma_toplevels_changed_cb (GListModel      *model,
+                           guint            position,
+                           guint            removed,
+                           guint            added,
+                           AdwStyleManager *self)
+{
+  luma_update_treatments (self);
+}
+
+static void
+luma_settings_changed_cb (GSettings       *settings,
+                          const char      *key,
+                          AdwStyleManager *self)
+{
+  luma_update_treatments (self);
+}
+
+static void
+luma_treatments_init (AdwStyleManager *self)
+{
+  if (self->display == NULL)
+    return;
+
+  self->luma_settings =
+    luma_optional_settings ("org.project_luma.shell-state", "surface-treatment");
+  self->luma_a11y_settings =
+    luma_optional_settings ("org.gnome.desktop.a11y.interface", "high-contrast");
+
+  if (self->luma_settings != NULL)
+    g_signal_connect (self->luma_settings, "changed",
+                      G_CALLBACK (luma_settings_changed_cb), self);
+
+  if (self->luma_a11y_settings != NULL)
+    g_signal_connect (self->luma_a11y_settings, "changed",
+                      G_CALLBACK (luma_settings_changed_cb), self);
+
+  self->luma_toplevels = gtk_window_get_toplevels ();
+  self->luma_toplevels_changed =
+    g_signal_connect (self->luma_toplevels, "items-changed",
+                      G_CALLBACK (luma_toplevels_changed_cb), self);
+
+  /* Above the theme, so the names it defines win, and below application
+   * composition, so an application can still say something of its own. */
+  self->luma_provider = gtk_css_provider_new ();
+  gtk_style_context_add_provider_for_display (self->display,
+                                              GTK_STYLE_PROVIDER (self->luma_provider),
+                                              GTK_STYLE_PROVIDER_PRIORITY_SETTINGS);
+
+  luma_update_treatments (self);
+}
+
 static void
 adw_style_manager_constructed (GObject *object)
 {
@@ -570,6 +827,8 @@
   update_fonts (self);
   update_stylesheet (self, UPDATE_ALL);
 
+  luma_treatments_init (self);
+
   if (self->provider) {
     gtk_css_provider_load_from_resource (self->provider,
                                          "/org/gnome/Adwaita/styles/gtk.css");
@@ -589,6 +848,17 @@
   g_clear_pointer (&self->document_font_name, g_free);
   g_clear_pointer (&self->monospace_font_name, g_free);
 
+  if (self->luma_toplevels != NULL && self->luma_toplevels_changed != 0)
+    g_clear_signal_handler (&self->luma_toplevels_changed, self->luma_toplevels);
+  g_clear_object (&self->luma_toplevels);
+  if (self->luma_settings != NULL)
+    g_signal_handlers_disconnect_by_data (self->luma_settings, self);
+  if (self->luma_a11y_settings != NULL)
+    g_signal_handlers_disconnect_by_data (self->luma_a11y_settings, self);
+  g_clear_object (&self->luma_provider);
+  g_clear_object (&self->luma_settings);
+  g_clear_object (&self->luma_a11y_settings);
+
   G_OBJECT_CLASS (adw_style_manager_parent_class)->dispose (object);
 }
 
diff --git a/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss b/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss
--- a/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss
+++ b/src/stylesheet/widgets/_header-bar.scss
@@ -782,3 +782,159 @@
     --dim-opacity: 90%;
   }
 }
+
+// One material, stated where every application can see it.
+//
+// Luma's palette reaches libadwaita as `@luma_*` named colours (0005). GTK
+// named colours are global: there is one set, and it cannot be restated per
+// window, so a Frost or Glass window carried the *light* values for every
+// role except the handful an application redefined for itself by loading the
+// kit's token sheet. Two consequences, both visible on one desktop:
+//
+//   * `@luma_chrome` was never defined here at all. The title band of 0040
+//     names it, so in an application that loads the kit the band is drawn and
+//     in an application that does not, GTK drops the declaration without a
+//     word and the band is missing. Same window, same treatment, two colours.
+//   * `.luma-island` paints `@luma_content`, which is opaque paper here and
+//     translucent in the kit's Frost and Glass sheets, so islands were
+//     see-through in one application and solid in the next.
+//
+// Custom properties are scoped to a selector, which named colours are not, so
+// the treatment's material is restated as properties on the window. Every
+// application then follows the appearance whether or not it knows the kit
+// exists, and there is one value per role rather than one per application.
+window.luma-treatment-frost {
+  --luma-veil: rgba(248, 249, 250, 0.90);
+  --luma-material-frame: rgba(242, 243, 245, 0.62);
+  --luma-paper: #fbfbfa;
+  --luma-paper-raised: #ffffff;
+  --luma-material-ink: #1f262d;
+}
+
+window.luma-treatment-glass {
+  --luma-veil: rgba(250, 250, 251, 0.85);
+  --luma-material-frame: rgba(255, 255, 255, 0.30);
+  --luma-paper: #fbfbfa;
+  --luma-paper-raised: #ffffff;
+  --luma-material-ink: #161b20;
+}
+
+// The title band. One layer, one colour, painted by the window and by nobody
+// else: the header bar and the top bar paint nothing (0041), so what a
+// titlebar is made of no longer depends on which widget an application put
+// there. 42px is the Luma title row.
+window.luma-treatment-frost,
+window.luma-treatment-glass,
+window.luma-treatment-frost:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass:backdrop {
+  background-image: linear-gradient(to bottom, var(--luma-veil), var(--luma-veil));
+  background-size: 100% 42px;
+  background-position: top;
+  background-repeat: no-repeat;
+}
+
+// Islands are opaque. The window's own surface is the material -- the frame,
+// the title band and the gaps between islands -- and the content surfaces
+// standing on it are paper, as they are in Light. A month grid or a sidebar
+// the wallpaper shows through is not a material; it is one text on top of
+// another.
+window.luma-treatment-frost .luma-island,
+window.luma-treatment-glass .luma-island,
+window.luma-treatment-frost .luma-island:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass .luma-island:backdrop {
+  background-color: var(--luma-paper);
+}
+
+window.luma-treatment-frost .luma-island.luma-raised,
+window.luma-treatment-glass .luma-island.luma-raised,
+window.luma-treatment-frost .luma-card,
+window.luma-treatment-glass .luma-card {
+  background-color: var(--luma-paper-raised);
+}
+
+// Floating surfaces -- context menus, popovers, sheets, the application menu
+// -- are the same veil as the title band, so a menu and a header read as one
+// material. They were 88% and 80% against the band's 37% and 46% before.
+window.luma-treatment-frost popover > contents,
+window.luma-treatment-glass popover > contents,
+window.luma-treatment-frost popover > arrow,
+window.luma-treatment-glass popover > arrow {
+  background-color: var(--luma-veil);
+}
+
+// Text on a translucent surface is plain text: the treatment's ink and
+// nothing behind it. A shadow or an outline behind a glyph is what a surface
+// reaches for when it is too thin to carry its own text. The veil is thick
+// enough now, so the rescue comes off everywhere rather than in the places
+// somebody happened to notice.
+window.luma-treatment-frost,
+window.luma-treatment-glass,
+window.luma-treatment-frost *,
+window.luma-treatment-glass * {
+  text-shadow: none;
+  -gtk-icon-shadow: none;
+}
+
+// The title row paints nothing of its own, whoever put it there.
+//
+// Three things were still painting it. Patch0041 zeroes --headerbar-bg-color
+// only on `window.csd`, and a window is not always `.csd` -- an X11 window
+// without a compositor is `solid-csd`, and then the light headerbar colour
+// came back. The stock sheet also paints `headerbar` with a light gradient
+// under prefers-color-scheme, which no custom property switches off. And
+// Patch0014 paints a kit window's title row `@luma_window` at a specificity
+// no plain `headerbar` rule reaches. So all three are named here.
+window.luma-treatment-frost,
+window.luma-treatment-glass {
+  --headerbar-bg-color: transparent;
+  --headerbar-backdrop-color: transparent;
+}
+
+window.luma-treatment-frost headerbar,
+window.luma-treatment-glass headerbar,
+window.luma-treatment-frost headerbar:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass headerbar:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-frost headerbar.luma-titlebar,
+window.luma-treatment-glass headerbar.luma-titlebar,
+window.luma-treatment-frost headerbar.luma-titlebar:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass headerbar.luma-titlebar:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-frost .titlebar,
+window.luma-treatment-glass .titlebar,
+window.luma-treatment-frost toolbarview > .top-bar,
+window.luma-treatment-glass toolbarview > .top-bar,
+window.luma-treatment-frost toolbarview > .top-bar:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass toolbarview > .top-bar:backdrop {
+  background: none;
+  background-color: transparent;
+  background-image: none;
+  box-shadow: none;
+}
+
+// The window's own surface is the material. Patch0014 and Patch0017 paint it
+// `@luma_window`, which is now the treatment's frame, so those rules are
+// already right; this covers a window neither of them names -- a plain
+// GtkApplicationWindow, or an AdwApplicationWindow an application built
+// itself -- which had libadwaita's opaque window colour.
+window.luma-treatment-frost,
+window.luma-treatment-glass,
+window.luma-treatment-frost:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass:backdrop {
+  background-color: unquote("@luma_window");
+}
+
+// Only the window paints the material. A kit window puts a toolbar view and a
+// padded body between the window and its islands; both took the window colour
+// as well, so the frame was painted twice and a kit window was a denser glass
+// than the window beside it -- 52% against 30%.
+window.luma-treatment-frost toolbarview,
+window.luma-treatment-glass toolbarview,
+window.luma-treatment-frost toolbarview:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass toolbarview:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-frost .luma-window-body,
+window.luma-treatment-glass .luma-window-body,
+window.luma-treatment-frost .luma-window-body:backdrop,
+window.luma-treatment-glass .luma-window-body:backdrop {
+  background: none;
+  background-color: transparent;
+  background-image: none;
+}
